Since you already have the VoucherWorkflow table tracking every step, you can easily pull the most recent record for a specific BATCH_ID and compare its LAST_ACTION_AT timestamp to the current time.
Here is the cleanest way to implement this in your Spring Boot application using your existing architecture.
### Step 1: Update Your Repository
Right now, your VoucherWorkflowRepository has a method that pulls *all* actions and sorts them ascending. Pulling the whole list just to check the last item wastes memory.
Add a new method that asks the database to sort descending and return **only the single most recent row**.
**File:** src/main/java/com/fincore/VoucherService/Repository/VoucherWorkflowRepository.java
```java
package com.fincore.VoucherService.Repository;

import com.fincore.VoucherService.Models.VoucherWorkflow;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Repository;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;

@Repository
public interface VoucherWorkflowRepository extends JpaRepository<VoucherWorkflow, Long> {
    
    // Your existing method
    List<VoucherWorkflow> findByBatchIdFkOrderByLastActionAtAsc(String batchIdFk);

    // NEW: Fetches only the single most recent workflow action instantly
    Optional<VoucherWorkflow> findTopByBatchIdFkOrderByLastActionAtDesc(String batchIdFk);
}

```
### Step 2: Calculate the Pending Time in Your Service
Wherever you are building your response for the frontend (likely in a Service class that builds your pending list), use Java's Duration class to calculate the exact time difference.
```java
import java.time.Duration;
import java.time.LocalDateTime;
import java.util.Optional;

// ... inside your Service class ...

public String getPendingTimeSinceLastAction(String batchId) {
    // 1. Fetch the most recent action for this batch
    Optional<VoucherWorkflow> latestActionOpt = 
        voucherWorkflowRepository.findTopByBatchIdFkOrderByLastActionAtDesc(batchId);

    // 2. If no actions exist yet, the pending time is 0 (or you can fallback to batch creation time)
    if (latestActionOpt.isEmpty() || latestActionOpt.get().getLastActionAt() == null) {
        return "0h 0m"; 
    }

    // 3. Get the timestamps
    LocalDateTime lastActionTime = latestActionOpt.get().getLastActionAt();
    LocalDateTime currentTime = LocalDateTime.now();

    // 4. Calculate the duration between the last action and right now
    Duration duration = Duration.between(lastActionTime, currentTime);

    // 5. Format the output cleanly (e.g., "2h 15m")
    long hours = duration.toHours();
    long minutes = duration.toMinutesPart(); // Gets the remaining minutes after hours are extracted

    return hours + "h " + minutes + "m";
}

```
### Why this approach is optimal:
 * **Database Efficiency:** The findTopBy...Desc naming convention translates to a highly optimized FETCH FIRST 1 ROW ONLY SQL query in Oracle. It doesn't load a massive list of history into your server's RAM.
 * **Precision:** By using java.time.Duration, you avoid the nightmare of doing manual milliseconds-to-minutes math.
 * **Accurate SLA Tracking:** If a batch was created 3 days ago, but the Level 1 approver just approved it 10 minutes ago, the Level 2 approver will correctly see "Pending since: 10m" instead of "Pending since: 3 days."
